   ctx="The 1890s: the decayed Maine port towns, the disappearance of the shipping trade, and the world of the unmarried and widowed women who remained.",
   sig="The Country of the Pointed Firs is the finest work of American regionalism and one of the most perfect books in American literature: its structure, its treatment of women's community and its rendering of the speech of the Maine coast made it the model for the American regional novel and a central text of feminist criticism.",
